+## Stdio proxy
+
+Clients that can start a process but cannot open the local socket directly can
+use the generic stdio bridge:
+
+```bash
+herdr --session work api proxy --stdio
+```
+
+The bridge accepts one public API request JSON object per stdin line. Ordinary
+responses are written unchanged as NDJSON on stdout, so their public `id` and
+error body remain authoritative. `events.subscribe` sends its normal
+`subscription_started` response first; each following subscription event is
+written as a small multiplexing envelope:
+
+```json
+{"type":"proxy.event","request_id":"subscribe-1","event":{"event":"pane.exited","data":{}}}
+```
+
+`request_id` is the public request id used to open that subscription. This is
+needed only because a single stdio process can carry more than one subscription;
+the nested `event` value is the unmodified public event envelope. The proxy
+reserves stderr for diagnostics and never writes diagnostics into stdout.
+
+The proxy does not accept an arbitrary socket path and never starts or stops a
+server. Use Herdr's standard `--session NAME` option or `HERDR_SESSION` to
+select the existing named session. EOF on stdin exits the proxy and releases
+only its socket connections; it does not stop Herdr or its panes.
+
